    "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-121424: intersections solve 8x²+2x−6=0, giving −1 and 3/4; upper minus lower is −8x²−2x+6. q-3121: intersections are −1/2 and 1, with height x+1−2x². q-11487: intersections are −1 and 5, with height 5+4x−x². q-49885: intersections solve 5x²+3x−2=0, giving −1 and 2/5; height is −5x²−3x+2. q-3146: integrating −x²−3x from −3 to 0 gives 9/2. q-11488: integrating 1−x² from −1 to 1 gives 4/3. q-9669: the determinant is 17−k, so singularity requires k=17. q-128478: det(F)=−2+48−39=7, so only II is true. q-65508: expanding √2 cos(8x−π/4) gives cos(8x)+sin(8x). q-11216: integrating 1−y⁵ from 0 to 1 gives 5/6. q-78892: the curve meets the y-axis at y=±2; integrating 2−y²/2 between those limits gives 16/3. q-117485: for y≥0 the bounded region extends from 0 to 3; integrating 9y−y³ gives 81/4. q-78896: inversion gives x=(y−1)³+1, whose integral from 0 to 1 is 3/4. q-115410: the denominator is strictly positive, so the inequality reduces to x²−4<0, giving −2<x<2. q-115429: division by the positive exponential gives 5x+6≥0, hence x≥−6/5. q-87772: division by 3^(4x)>0 gives x(x−3)≥0, hence x≤0 or x≥3. No earlier submitted answer or grade is supplied; confirmation rests on the captured worked solutions and independent mathematics.",
